And `SSL_CTX_set1_sigalgs` calls `parse_sigalg_pairs`:

```cpp
static bool parse_sigalg_pairs(Array<uint16_t> *out, const int *values,
                               size_t num_values) {
  if ((num_values & 1) == 1) {
    return false;
  }

  const size_t num_pairs = num_values / 2;
  if (!out->Init(num_pairs)) {
    return false;
  }

  for (size_t i = 0; i < num_values; i += 2) {
    const int hash_nid = values[i];
    const int pkey_type = values[i+1];

    bool found = false;
    for (const auto &candidate : kSignatureAlgorithmsMapping) {
      if (candidate.pkey_type == pkey_type && candidate.hash_nid == hash_nid) {
        (*out)[i / 2] = candidate.signature_algorithm;
        found = true;
        break;
      }
    }

    if (!found) {
      OPENSSL_PUT_ERROR(SSL, SSL_R_INVALID_SIGNATURE_ALGORITHM);
      ERR_add_error_dataf("unknown hash:%d pkey:%d", hash_nid, pkey_type);
      return false;
    }
  }

  return true;
}
```

If `num_values` is 0, then `num_pairs = 0`, `out->Init(0)` returns true with an empty array, and the loop doesn't execute. This seems fine.

But wait - what if `num_values` is even but non-zero, and some pair is invalid? Then the function returns false, and `*out` is not modified. But `out->Init(num_pairs)` was called, which allocated memory. When the function returns false, the caller `SSL_CTX_set1_sigalgs` returns 0. The `sigalgs` array is destroyed. This seems fine.

